
Firstly, this file is purely a small aid file intended on getting you
going with the ACDriver.  You should also read the "ACDriver.DOC" file to
understand just how the MENUS work..

There are MANY powerful things that can be achieved with the ACDriver
and AML, that will come in future .. for now get the basics going
before the other major points are explained.

1. Making ApT use the ACDriver:

		'SMALLCMD' "ACDRIVER"

It is as  simple as that - The ACDriver is then used.

2. assigning new assigns:

   aptNews: 		points to the directory that is to hold all of the
						bulletins,news items etc.

	aptGeneral:		the general files directory (gfiles)

	modules:			program files, (games and so forth..)


	NOTE: The above "assign names" will most probably change (especially
	the modules: one..) somtime soon.

3. Creating a new directory

	make quite sure that you create a directory called "tplates:acd" where
	items of the ACDriver nature will be placed. The thing to note about
	this directory is that it will probably contain files that do not
	always use the .tpl extension like most of the other dirs in the tplates:
	section.

	The following files are purely "display enhance" files, and are not
	really required. Entries within the "acdBBSText" file to these
	files can be removed if required. Generally hunting for the
	REXX.$$'showfile' etc.. text will locate them.

	termprf_frame.tpl            
	time_frame.tpl               
	persprf_frame.tpl            
	status_frame.tpl             

	MENUS:

	lists                       : Use for News/Gfiles/Pfiles
	mainmenu                    : Quite obviously your main menu via ?
	olm                         : Used whenever ? inside of an OLM is used
	filesmain                   : ? inside of the files area menu
	msg                         : ? inside of the REXX "Gateway.AML" script

	HELP:

	create a directory called "tplates:apthelp" - This directory will be
	then used to contain additional help files that can be displayed
	via use of the HELP <helpfile> function. The help-files that are
	included should be changed (a wink should indicate just why ;-)
	and besides, they are not complete yet as all of the commands
	available do not yet have help files.. (as they should)

4. An additional "REXX" script exists (that I use on my system, NOT supplied)
	which "kind of" gives the feel of the message bases being part of the 
	ACDriver	system with use of keywords etc.. Thus keeping the effect going.
	It has to be 'tailored' to each individuals system and does require
	prior knowledge of arexx and your message base areas. It's not
	for the faint hearted I might add 8-) but can be supplied for those
	who would like to see what is possible.

Your OTHER menu-sets can be made to use portions of the ACDriver
by using the following arexx commands (used with the SMALLCMD function),
and some of the commands listed (such as SCANNEWS) should be used in
early portions of your login sequence.

SCANNEWS

   Scans for new News and displays any to the user. This should be used
   in some early part of your "login script" (just after they have
   logged in)

WRITEOLM

	Allows the user to write an OLM to anothe user who might be online.

WHO

   Allows you to check just WHO is online.

SHOWSTATS

   Same as the ACDriver version

FINGER

   Speaks for itself .. If you specify a LineNumber, or a Handle, or
   a UserName all of the "lines" are scanned and any that match "that users"
   information will be displayed, otherwise data would be pulled from a
   folder, as long as you specify a valid full name.

	If the SHOWBEFORE command is also supplied then the handle/name/line
	the user has supplied will be displayed (prompted) and return requested
	(or some editing) before the command does its "thing.."
	
SHOWBEFORE

   Supplied with the Finger (possibly others in future..)

NEWS

	Enters the Bulletins (News) Room where all of the actual news items
	are written and "exist" for re-reading. At present "ED" is called
	whenever an item is being posted/edited/written (text files) - This
	is because there are no "decent" editors for the BBS yet that allow
	files to be "loaded into them" .. 

PFILES:

	Displays the Program Files (doors, games, utilities..)

GFILES:

	General files, (text files, documentation, information)

ACDRIVER:

	Use the "entire" ACDriver as the control method for the system.
